INT. NEONATAL ICU - NIGHT

Soft blue light. Incubators like glass bread boxes. Every monitor beats in a different key.

GUNNAR ASPLUND, 50s, charge nurse, moves between the cribs checking tubes. FATIMA HADDAD, 30s, neonatologist, writes on a chart with a stub of pencil. OLUWASEUN "SEUN" ADEBOYE, 20s, resident, sips from a paper cup. MARIBEL SOTELO, 40s, a mother in a hospital gown, sits motionless beside incubator six, her hand pressed flat to the glass.

Gunnar adjusts a drip. Fatima checks a number, writes it, checks it again. Seun yawns into the cup. Maribel hums, tuneless.

GUNNAR
Bed six, stable.

FATIMA
Bed six, thank you.

SEUN
Is she asleep?

GUNNAR
She's looking.

SEUN
She's not allowed to look at the monitor.

FATIMA
(over Seun)
Let her.

MARIBEL
(over Fatima)
He moved his foot.

GUNNAR
He did.

FATIMA
He did, Mrs. Sotelo. That's very good.

SEUN
(to Gunnar)
Do I say it's good?

GUNNAR
It's good.

SEUN
(to Maribel)
It's very good.

Maribel presses her forehead to the glass. Her breath fogs it. Gunnar reaches over and wipes the fog away with his sleeve.

He crosses to bed four. She studies the chart again. He yawns again. The monitors continue their small, stubborn song. Fatima glances up at the mother, then at the baby, then writes one more number in the corner of the chart and underlines it twice.
